WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  lorraine here speaking with vfx  supervisor of marvel studios one  division tara demarco hey tara hi  lorraine it's so great to get to talk to  you i'm so thrilled to talk about this  show because it is such a love letter to  classic television yeah kevin feige  really wanted us at every turn to stay  as authentic to period filmmaking as we  could was there anything that you found  really surprising about working in black  and white the most surprising thing  about working in black and white is then  looking at the color because you'll see  some crazy colors that were done with  our production design and our costume  department because they all knew they  would film better so  red films quite dark on the sensor it  just goes super super dark and  unreadable we were losing visions  features so we did two days of makeup  palette testing where we tried all  different shades of blue and different  shades of sheen because some of that  performance we keep like some of that  face is is the real face and we augment  it with cg clearly but paul as an actor  is so expressive and particularly when  he was channeling his sick himself he  was so expressive and we didn't want to  lose any of that early on in the season  we get to see wanda in her 1950s  paradise she is having a lot of magical  fun in the kitchen  where did you decide what's going to be  practical and what's going to be vfx we  would all sit around a table and and  decide as a group we would decide with  props and special effects and production  design and talk about the dimensions of  the set and what we wanted the effect to  be and what we wanted  wanted to see or feel do we need the  actors to be able to see that thing  practically moving through space or did  it just appear so we would all discuss  the parameters  and then we would dive in and figure out  like okay well  this one thing would be great on wires  because there's no way you're going to  be able to cheat the effect of a  puppeteer like bobbling an item through  space like that's a surprisingly hard  thing to do in cg is recreate the hand  of an old school puppeteer working on  wires it's pretty wild to see the  footage from behind the scenes because  you don't realize how close the  puppeteers are what were some of the  ways that items were puppeteered all of  them so we  we puppeteered things from the ceiling  we  did them from the floor we  tried all of the ways so really it was  about looking okay what what's the best  way to get the effect we want and then  how do you rig that it's like oh will  you put a wire from one side of the  living room through the set window and  then have someone pull it across so once  you have all of this wonderful  puppeteering  uh put together with the actors in their  scene  how do you take that into vfx and make  it all one seamless product we take all  of our in-camera elements all of the  pieces with puppeteered wires still in  them and then get to work at removing  all of those wires and rods and  everything else and then  we take that scene as prepped and go to  cg  with our director and he might say can i  have a rolling pin or can you make me  one more of those cookie jars and then  we'll begin modeling those items with  colors that are cg friendly for black  and white and we'll lay them out in the  scene do animation that  is janky enough that it could have been  done by a human with a rod or a wire we  didn't want anything too smooth because  then we would give up the gag with  everything that was done with pliers  [Music]  i especially love the moment where wanda  finds out that she's pregnant and the  whole room goes from black and white  into this bloom of color where did you  start with this process when you knew  you had to take the world into color we  were really encouraged by the studio to  look at film effects that could have  happened in the era  and so  the 60s the film effects that could  happen in the era were all about optical  printing and how you could expose  different bits of film and then  re-expose them together in an optical  printer  and we took inspiration from that and  thought  what if you separated out the color  channels  and really warped them to be separate  from one another and then were able to  re-expose them and layer them  and that's the effect that creates the  color bloom and so we ended up with an  effect that would have been tricky to do  in the 60s but at least was true to the  principles and then our little stars  that are animated around were a nod to  animating on the film from that era  [Music]  so when wanda and vision are getting  into nesting mode setting up the nursery  there's some really charming moments  that happen  as you know things start to literally  take shape what were some of the ways  that that was shot to trick the eye the  crib coming together was filmed with  wires with puppeteers  the paint brushes we did both ways there  were some puppeteered paintbrushes that  i think ultimately we used for reference  and we did cg paint brushes in that  scene there's also some wonderful  moments my favorite being the butterfly  mobile what was the initial idea for how  much of that you could really do  practically as butterflies i don't  imagine are super cooperative  butterflies are not super cooperative  but one of the butterflies on paul's  nose is real and the others are all cg  so we wanted at least one so that we  could see how it moved and see what it  looked like and see that contact and  actually just the wonder in their faces  when we had this real live butterfly  attached to paul's nose pretty  incredible and then  what we decided to do was animate the  butterflies and and that allowed us that  opened us up to make it more fantastical  because if we had had real butterflies  you know they have phenomenal colors but  they might not exactly match our nursery  mobile and this allowed us to art direct  them a little closer to the mobile and  get that perfect transition moment  [Music]  so we have the epic moment where vision  meets vision how do you  make them look  like they're both vision while giving  them their own distinct look the new  vision was designed by marvel's very own  biz dev team he has an identical body  but a slightly different costume he has  an identical head but slightly different  panel shapes and we gave him an  interesting new synthesiod eye things  that are important differences the new  vision doesn't have a mind stone  so we put  something mind stone-esque in  the area where the mind stone was  so the interesting thing that comes with  filming this kind of scene you have two  visions you have one paul bettany  how do you then uh  shoot that scene to put it together in  post we shoot the scene twice so we shot  every scene with  paul  as the hero and then he would act  opposite one of his stunt doubles so  that every performance is paul so once  you have two paws and you've got him  into one shot uh how do you then create  a logic battle we take all of the  elements from the set we'll cut them all  out and assemble them into scenes so  that we feel like it's all one scene and  then we figure out what additional  enhancements need to happen so that they  really sit together does it mean a  little bit of set extension or some  atmosphere or tracking one vision into  the other's original photography so that  they share a camera move whatever we  need to do to make them both feel like  they're really there and they're in a  library how how was that shot was that a  library did you create one we scouted a  library that ultimately we decided we  couldn't shoot there because we needed  to hang them quite high and this little  library didn't really want us to destroy  their space so we  found it loved it photographed it and  then we went back to our stage and built  three quarters of it for real so that we  could practically hang and spin them on  the second floor so that we could get  the shots from down below of them  talking and spinning and so we could get  some shots drifting with them as they're  on the second floor  ultimately we ended up rebuilding a  bunch of that library in cg including  the dome and the skylight just so we  would have full control but it was  massively helpful to have a real set and  a lot of that set lives in the final  film are there any layers or  different things that you put on top to  raise the stakes of that feeling  absolutely we  put  bits of paper and laser beams we have  exploding wood and we cut through the  floor so you end up with lots of stuff  in the air and some stuff coming towards  lens that helps the viewer feel like  they're really in the middle of this  tense battle beat looking back at this  process working on this entire series  the first disney plus series for the mcu  uh what has been sort of your favorite  component or part of getting to work on  this process i think my favorite part of  working on this process  has been working with the director in  the studio in the new character  development every time someone else is  introduced  i'm like oh who is that and how cool is  it and now we've been able to contribute  to that thank you so much tara it was so  wonderful talking to you everyone be  sure to watch marvel studios one  division now streaming all episodes on  disney plus  [Music]

